<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="tricin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is a flavonoid typically distributed in sedges, palms, and grasses, including cereal crops such as rice (|FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565 &lt;i&gt;Triticum aestivum&lt;/i&gt;|), barley (|FRAME: TAX-4513 &lt;i&gt;Hordeum vulgare&lt;/i&gt;|), maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577 &lt;i&gt;Zea mays&lt;/i&gt;|), and sorghum (|FRAME: TAX-4558 &lt;i&gt;Sorghum bicolor&lt;/i&gt;|) |CITS: [Zhou10]|. It is found mostly as &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-linked conjugates that serve as allelochemicals |CITS: [20730995]| and insect deterrents |CITS: [Ling07]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is also found as a flavonoid monomer in monocot lignin, representing the first non-monolignol monomer involved in lignification |CITS: [25667313]|. Tricin is considered a major candidate to be developed as a nutraceutical because of its antioxidant |CITS: [17350657]|, anticancer |CITS: [17089164]|, anti-inflammatory |CITS: [22705359]|, and cardiovascular-protective properties |CITS: [20006366]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is produced in rice from the flavanone |FRAME: NARINGENIN-CMPD (2&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-naringenin|, which is converted into the flavone |FRAME: CPD-431 apigenin|. Two enzymatically distinct flavone synthases occur in plants - the soluble Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;/2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase |FRAME: EC-1.14.20.5 EC 1.14.20.5, flavone synthase I|, and the cytochrome P-450 |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.76 EC 1.14.19.76, flavone synthase II|. It has been shown that only the latter contributes to tricin formation, forming |CITS: [26082402]|. |FRAME: CPD-431 apigenin| is hydroxylated at the C-3&apos; position, followed by methylation of the hydroxyl group to form |FRAME: 574-TRIHYDROXY-3-METHOXYFLAVONE chrysoeriol|. The main enzyme responsible for the hydroxylation is the cytochrmome P-450 |FRAME: G-58315 CYP75B4|, as a mutation in this gene results in complete absence of |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| derivatives |CITS: [26082402]|. The methylation can be catalyzed by a number of &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferases, such as |FRAME: G-58316 ROMT-9| |CITS: [26082402]| and |FRAME: G-58318 ROMT-15| |CITS: [17943312]|. |FRAME: G-58315 CYP75B4| then catalyzes an additional hydroxylation, this time at position 5&apos;, forming |FRAME: CPD-12571 selgin|, which is methylated to |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| |CITS: [26082402]|. Note that |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| can also be formed from |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin| by the action of |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.175 EC 2.1.1.175, tricin synthase|, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6631 tricetin methylation|.</Description>
